This test writes an MP4 using PyAV with the "h264" encoder. In environments where `av` is installed but the underlying FFmpeg build lacks H.264 encoding support (common in minimal CI images), `container.add_stream("h264", ...)` can raise and fail the suite. Consider skipping when H.264 encoding isn’t available (probe codec support / catch the encoder error and `pytest.skip`), or use a more broadly available codec for the synthetic MP4.
```suggestion
        try:
            with av.open(buf, mode="w", format="mp4") as container:
                stream = container.add_stream("h264", rate=fps)
                stream.width = width
                stream.height = height
                stream.pix_fmt = "yuv420p"
                for _ in range(num_frames):
                    frame = av.VideoFrame.from_ndarray(
                        np.zeros((height, width, 3), dtype=np.uint8), format="rgb24"
                    )
                    for packet in stream.encode(frame):
                        container.mux(packet)
                for packet in stream.encode():
                    container.mux(packet)
        except Exception as exc:
            pytest.skip(f"H.264 encoding support is unavailable in this PyAV/FFmpeg build: {exc}")
```

This was added to address this bugbot concern:

> `GroupByLabelBatchSampler.__iter__` only applies seed when `self.generator` is set, but still uses randomness (`torch.randperm`) even when `self.generator` is None. This makes the `seed` argument ineffective in the common case where no generator is passed, reducing reproducibility and potentially changing training behavior between runs.

