The context manager doesn't handle the case where an attribute doesn't exist initially. When `getattr(cls, name, None)` returns None, it's ambiguous whether the attribute didn't exist or had a value of None. In the finally block, `setattr(cls, name, value)` will set the attribute to None even if it didn't exist before. Consider using `hasattr` to check existence and `delattr` to remove attributes that didn't exist initially, rather than setting them to None.
```suggestion
    originals: dict[str, Any] = {}
    existed: dict[str, bool] = {}
    for name in overrides:
        if hasattr(cls, name):
            existed[name] = True
            originals[name] = getattr(cls, name)
        else:
            existed[name] = False
    try:
        for name, value in overrides.items():
            setattr(cls, name, value)
        yield
    finally:
        for name in overrides:
            if existed.get(name, False):
                setattr(cls, name, originals[name])
            else:
                if hasattr(cls, name):
                    delattr(cls, name)
```

